s1-p16 = realized (prose (cons "**A reversible whole with no observed law.** Take \(J=\mathsf{Bool}\times\mathsf{Bool}\), the global step \(s(a,b)=(a\oplus b,b)\), and the observation \(o(a,b)=a\). The step is its own inverse: \(s(s(a,b))=(a,b)\). But there is no \(g:\mathsf{Bool}\to\mathsf{Bool}\) satisfying \(g(o(j))=o(s(j))\) for every \(j:J\). At \(j=(\mathsf{true},\mathsf{false})\), such a \(g\) would give \(g(\mathsf{true})=\mathsf{true}\); at \(j=(\mathsf{true},\mathsf{true})\), it would give \(g(\mathsf{true})=\mathsf{false}\). The contradiction proves that the visible coordinate has no autonomous next-state function under this observation, although the joint evolution loses nothing. The problem is not the absence of a global law but the failure of that law to *descend* through the observation. The example also makes the observer-relative content of a law precise: what matters is the existence of a commuting map on the observed states, not merely the existence of a law on the whole. The same question about descent recurs for source-language projections and distributed views." nil)) (cons (cite bxor "bxor") (cons (cite whole-step "whole-step") (cons (cite whole-step-involution "whole-step-involution") (cons (cite no-observed-law "no-observed-law") nil))))
